# modules/dast/passive/checks.rules - the §7.1 passive script-check registry
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md tier 2).
#
# This is the passive tier's first checks.rules.  checks_registry_load globs
# every *.rules under modules/dast/ at any depth, so this file sits beside the
# scripts it registers rather than in a module-root registry, exactly as
# modules/dast/active/checks.rules already does for the injection tier.
#
# DAST-05..DAST-11 are peer tickets with no ordering between them and each owns
# its own FAMILY component (§9.1.1), so each can add its records to this file
# without colliding: `BANNER` here, and headers.sh / cookies.sh / tls.sh /
# cors.sh / leakage.sh / markup.sh bring their own.
#
# The type tag is `passive`, matching the phase table's
# `passive/banner.sh:passive` floor (modules/dast/engine.sh): these run at every
# intensity, including the default.  They are also tagged `quick`, which is the
# only §9.1.3 profile tag that means anything for them - `--profile-scan quick`
# is "passive + config-read, no active probes" (docs/DESIGN.md §5), and a
# read-only look at a response the run was going to make anyway is exactly that.
#
# THREE IDS RATHER THAN ONE, and the reason is the fingerprint, not taste.  The
# DAST location profile is (target, method, path_template, param_location,
# param_name) and carries no "kind" component, so a single id would make a
# name-only disclosure and a known-vulnerable-version hit on the same endpoint
# collide and dedupe to one - the same collision modules/dast/active/checks.rules
# records for the three SQLi techniques.  They also carry genuinely different
# severities, CWEs and OWASP categories, which one record cannot.

id: DAST-BANNER-SERVER_DISCLOSURE-01
title: Server or framework disclosed in a response header
script: passive/banner.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Suppress or overwrite the product token in the response -
  `server_tokens off` (nginx), `ServerTokens Prod` with `ServerSignature Off`
  (Apache), `expose_php = Off` (PHP), or removing `X-Powered-By` in the
  application framework - and strip it at the reverse proxy or CDN where the
  origin cannot be changed. Treat this as defence in depth and patch the
  component on its own schedule regardless.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-BANNER-VERSION_DISCLOSURE-01
title: Framework or component version disclosed
script: passive/banner.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Stop publishing the exact version to unauthenticated clients:
  suppress the product token in the response header, remove the generator meta
  tag the CMS or static-site generator emits, and serve bundles under a
  content-hashed filename rather than a version-numbered one. Keep the
  component patched as well, because a version an attacker can fingerprint by
  behaviour is only hidden, not fixed.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: DAST-BANNER-OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01
title: Component version named in the vendored known-vulnerable list
script: passive/banner.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-1104
owasp: A06:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Upgrade the component to a release the advisory does not name, or
  apply the vendor backport for it. Where an upgrade cannot be immediate, put a
  compensating control in front of the specific weakness the advisory describes
  and track the upgrade as the remediation rather than treating the control as
  one. Re-read the running version from the same surface afterwards.
references: CWE-1104
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A06_2021-Vulnerable_and_Outdated_Components/
